WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.044
A discarded sofa can block a sidewalk. When residents report illegal dumping to San

00:00:05.044 --> 00:00:09.905
José three one one, those reports reveal a pattern. Could last week’s calls help

00:00:09.905 --> 00:00:11.607
forecast next week’s demand?

00:00:12.167 --> 00:00:17.092
Our team mapped reports to ten San José council districts. For this case, follow

00:00:17.092 --> 00:00:22.140
District Three. Each Monday, we forecast how many new illegal dumping reports that

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:24.110
district will receive by Sunday.

00:00:24.667 --> 00:00:29.674
Before July twenty ninth, District Three had recorded one hundred fifteen, one

00:00:29.674 --> 00:00:34.809
hundred sixty-nine, one hundred sixteen, and one hundred twenty-one reports over

00:00:34.809 --> 00:00:38.661
four weeks. That averages about one hundred thirty per week.

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:43.827
Our Poisson model combined that recent history with a longer average, the district,

00:00:43.827 --> 00:00:48.459
and the time of year. Its forecast for the coming week was about one hundred twenty-

00:00:48.459 --> 00:00:49.231
three reports.

00:00:49.792 --> 00:00:54.592
By Sunday, two hundred twenty-seven reports had arrived. That was about one hundred

00:00:54.592 --> 00:00:59.277
four more than forecast. The anchored chart shows how sharply demand exceeded the

00:00:59.277 --> 00:01:01.243
level suggested by recent history.

00:01:01.833 --> 00:01:06.625
Across all ten districts and fifty-two validation weeks, the model missed by about

00:01:06.625 --> 00:01:11.299
fourteen reports per district-week on average. District Three shows why a weekly

00:01:11.299 --> 00:01:15.507
forecast is useful for planning, and why a surge still needs a response.
